{"subjectId":"python","id":"inheritance","title":"Inheritance, MRO & super()","group":"Object-Oriented Python","order":32,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">A subclass reuses and specialises its parent. Python supports multiple inheritance and resolves methods with a predictable order — the MRO.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-hierarchy-2\"></i>Inheritance &amp; overriding</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>class Animal:\n    def __init__(self, name):\n        self.name = name\n    def speak(self):\n        return \"...\"\n    def describe(self):\n        return f\"{self.name} says {self.speak()}\"\n\nclass Dog(Animal):\n    def __init__(self, name, breed):\n        super().__init__(name)           # let the parent set up its part\n        self.breed = breed\n    def speak(self):                     # override\n        return \"Woof\"\n\nd = Dog(\"Rex\", \"lab\")\nprint(d.describe())                              # Rex says Woof\nprint(isinstance(d, Animal), issubclass(Dog, Animal))   # True True</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p><code>describe</code> lives in <code>Animal</code> but calls <code>self.speak()</code> — Python looks it up on the actual object, so the <code>Dog</code> version runs. That's polymorphism.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-git-merge\"></i>Multiple inheritance &amp; the MRO</h3>\n<div class=\"callout callout--def\"><p><strong>MRO (Method Resolution Order)</strong> — the order in which classes are searched for an attribute, computed with <em>C3 linearization</em>. See it with <code>Class.__mro__</code> or <code>Class.mro()</code>.</p></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>class A:\n    def hello(self): return \"A\"\nclass B(A):\n    def hello(self): return \"B→\" + super().hello()\nclass C(A):\n    def hello(self): return \"C→\" + super().hello()\nclass D(B, C):\n    def hello(self): return \"D→\" + super().hello()\n\nprint(D().hello())                          # D→B→C→A\nprint([k.__name__ for k in D.__mro__])      # ['D', 'B', 'C', 'A', 'object']</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p><code>super()</code> means “the <strong>next class in the MRO</strong>”, not “my parent”. In <code>D(B, C)</code>, <code>B</code>'s <code>super()</code> goes to <code>C</code>, not <code>A</code> — that's what makes cooperative multiple inheritance work.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-template\"></i>Abstract base classes</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>from abc import ABC, abstractmethod\n\nclass Shape(ABC):\n    @abstractmethod\n    def area(self) -&gt; float: ...\n\nclass Square(Shape):\n    def __init__(self, side):\n        self.side = side\n    def area(self):\n        return self.side ** 2\n\nprint(Square(3).area())          # 9\ntry:\n    Shape()\nexcept TypeError:\n    print(\"can't instantiate\")   # can't instantiate</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-puzzle\"></i>Duck typing &amp; composition</h3>\n<div class=\"grid\"><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-feather\"></i>Duck typing</h3><p>“If it walks like a duck…” — code relies on <em>what an object can do</em> (has <code>read()</code>, is iterable) rather than its class.</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-components\"></i>Composition over inheritance</h3><p>Hold other objects as attributes (<code>self.engine = Engine()</code>) instead of building deep class trees.</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-blend-mode\"></i>Mixins</h3><p>Small classes adding one capability (e.g. <code>JsonMixin.to_json</code>) combined through multiple inheritance.</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-file-certificate\"></i>Protocols</h3><p><code>typing.Protocol</code> describes the methods a type needs — static duck typing for type checkers.</p></div></div></section>"}